==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|LL.|recapitulātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|[[recapitulo|recapitulāre]]||to go over the main points of a thing again}}, from {{af|la|re-|capitulum|t1=again|t2=head, main part, chapter}}, from {{suffix|la|caput|ulus|t1=[[head]]|pos2=diminutive suffix|alt2=ulum}}; see {{m|en|capitulate}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Canada|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɹiːkəˈpɪtʃʊleɪt/}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[summarize]] or repeat in [[concise]] form.
#: ''The entire symphony was '''recapitulated''' in the last four bars.''
#: ''We still have five minutes left, so let's '''recapitulate'''.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To reproduce or closely resemble (as in structure or function).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} {{lb|en|biology|of an organism}} To [[mirror]] or repeat in [[analogous]] form, especially in reference to an [[individual|individual's]] [[development]] passing through stages corresponding to the [[species|species']] stages of [[evolutionary]] development.
#* '''1997''', G. A. Bray, "Growth of a Molecular Base for Feeding," ''Obesity Research'', vol. 5, no. 3 (May), p. 272:
#*: Similarly this concept of unity provided a powerful impetus for embryological studies and the idea that fetal development '''recapitulates''' the steps of phylogenetic development.

